    "_what": "Two spans in order, so the song starts on the drum smash, loops back to its own top, and never plays the breakdown. Owner: 'we want the song on loop basically but starting at the drum smash', and 'cut out the weird drum section with the moaning'.",
    "segments": [
      {
        "_what": "A -- the drum smash to the last audible window. 193.420 is the MEASURED re-entry after the breakdown, +12.98 dB, the largest onset in the song. 240.780 is where the music actually ends: the file carries ~4.4 s of digital silence after it, and cutting on the file end would put the loop seam in the middle of it (issue #105).",
        "start_sec": 193.42,
        "end_sec": 240.78
      },
      {
        "_what": "B -- the top of the song to the start of the breakdown, which is the part the loop exists to let people hear. 181.320 is where the level falls to about -13 dB and stays; ending here removes the 12.10 s 'moaning' section. The bed record's own committed excision found the same span independently (removed_sec 12.097596).",
        "start_sec": 0.0,
        "end_sec": 181.32
      }
    ],
    "crossfade_sec": 0.25
  },
  "reveal": {
    "_what": "THE REAL REVEAL, and the owner named its time: ':22 is when I want the comic book shot, put the cast after'. 22.080 rather than a flat 22.000 because a measured +5.01 dB onset sits there -- 0.080 s, under three frames, so the cut lands on a hit instead of mid-bar. The earlier anchor (the song's opening crescendo, 9.080 s into span B) is kept below: it is why the loop exists, and it is what to fall back to if `at_sec` is ever removed.",
    "hold_sec": 14.0,
    "image": "~/Pictures/Artwork/wolves.jpg",
